{
  "case_id": "2020-18-540",
  "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nNICHOLAS J. BRONNI, Solicitor General, Little Rock, Arkansas; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nFREDERICK LIU, Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; for the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting the Petitioner.\nSETH P. WAXMAN, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Leslie Rutledge, Attorney General of Arkansas (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Pharmacy Benefit Managers are drug middlemen that reimburse pharmacists for the cost of prescription drugs. Those reimbursements are frequently below a pharmacist's cost. That drives pharmacists out of business, and it has left many communities without a pharmacist."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Leslie Rutledge, Attorney General of Arkansas (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Second, ERISA doesn't preempt laws that implement or enforce rate regulation. Indeed, absent enforcement, there's no regulation. And Respondent doesn't dispute that Act 900's enforcement mechanisms implement Arkansas's rate regulation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Following up on the Chief Justice's question, it seems that if the -- the pharmacy wins its appeal, that it has to rebill. And that seems that there's -- then it determines when the copay is -- determination is made final. So that seems to be something, the copay determination, that you would normally expect the plan to -- to decide. So isn't that something central to the plan?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: General, I had understood the argument in your briefs to extend further than the SG's argument. The SG spends a good deal of time talking about the distinction between regulating PBMs and regulating plans. So, if the plan managed prescription drug benefits itself, the SG says that would be a different question and we shouldn't reach that question."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: And can you explain that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Bronni",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We are requiring, I guess you could say, PBMs to reallocate some of that margin back to -- to local pharmacies in order to ensure that they can remain in business and so that small-town, independent rural pharmacies across our state, people in those communities retain access to pharmacies, because, when a small-town pharmacy closes in Hampton or Gillette, Arkansas, it might be 30 miles to get a drug, it might be 30 miles to get an immunization."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Bronni",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "At the end of the day, ERISA doesn't preempt state rate regulation, and Act 900 is state rate regulation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF FREDERICK LIU FOR THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, SUPPORTING THE PETITIONER"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Frederick Liu",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0001-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The key question in this case is whether the Arkansas law directly regulates a central matter of plan administration. If it does, then the law has an impermissible connection with ERISA plans."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0001-0007",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF SETH P. WAXMAN ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Waxman",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0001-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Act 900 directly compels ERISA plan administrators to comply with state-specific rules and procedures in administering their benefits programs. In doing so, it adds to a thicket of varying state laws that make uniform plan administration impossible."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "J.B. Hunt Company",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0002-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I commend the Court to the amicus brief filed by J.B. Hunt Company, which is an Arkansas employer that employs -- employ -- that employs drivers all over the country. It has a pretty good explication of how impossible uniform plan administration would be."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2020-18-540",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
      "evidence_id": "ev-699224459d84-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: If -- if ERISA preempts the law here, should we worry that it also preempts other sorts of general regulations about other kinds of benefits? This follows up on Justice Kagan's line of questioning."
    },
    {
